// Original questions (verbatim text) in compact form: [module_id, heading, [[question, answer], ...]]
export const MODULES = [
[1, "React-Basics - I", [
["What is React? What is the Role of React in software development?", "React is an open-source front-end JavaScript library developed by Meta. Its primary role in software development is to build dynamic, high-performance User Interfaces (UIs) using a component-based architecture."],
["What are the Key Features of React?", "Key features include Component-Based Architecture, Virtual DOM, JSX (JavaScript XML), Unidirectional Data Flow, and a robust ecosystem supporting state management and routing."],
["What is DOM? What is the difference between HTML and DOM?", "The DOM (Document Object Model) is a programming interface representing web pages as a tree structure of objects. HTML is the textual document format used to write layouts, while the DOM is the active, live object tree memory representation generated by the browser from that HTML."],
["What is Virtual DOM? Difference between DOM and Virtual DOM?", "The Virtual DOM is a lightweight, in-memory copy of the real DOM. When updates occur, React alters the Virtual DOM first, runs a diffing algorithm, and patches only the changed nodes onto the real DOM, making it significantly faster than direct real DOM manipulation."],
["What are React Components? What are the main elements of it?", "React Components are reusable, self-contained UI building blocks. The main elements composing a component are State (internal memory), Props (external configuration inputs), and a Return statement specifying the layout via JSX."],
["What is SPA(Single Page Application)?", "An SPA is a web application that loads a single HTML page. Subsequent interaction dynamically rewrites the page via JavaScript API calls rather than forcing the browser to download entirely new pages from a server."],
["What are the 5 Advantages of React?", "1. Faster performance via Virtual DOM. 2. Component reusability saves time. 3. SEO-friendly implementation via Server-Side Rendering tools. 4. Strong developer community backing. 5. Unidirectional data flow ensures predictable debugging."],
["What are the Disadvantages of React?", "High pace of development means documentation updates can lag, JSX can present a steep learning curve for newer developers, and it is a library rather than a full framework, requiring third-party tools for routing and complex state operations."],
["What is the role of JSX in React? (3 points)", "1. It allows developers to write visual HTML structures directly inside JavaScript files. 2. It boosts performance by converting tags into optimized React element creation functions. 3. It prevents XSS injection attacks by escaping values automatically before rendering."],
["What is the difference between Declarative & Imperative syntax?", "Imperative syntax explicitly states the step-by-step instructions required to achieve a state (e.g., manual DOM operations). Declarative syntax describes *what* the final UI state should look like, leaving the underlying execution management up to the library/framework."],
]],
[2, "React-Basics - II", [
["What is Arrow Function Expression in JSX?", "An inline arrow function expression `() => doSomething()` used inside JSX event attributes (like `onClick`) to evaluate statements or pass arguments gracefully on interaction."],
["How to Setup React first project?", "You can establish a new project using modern toolchains by running `npm create vite@latest my-app -- --template react` followed by `npm install` and `npm run dev` inside your terminal."],
["How React App Load and display the components in browser?", "The browser runs compiled JS assets. The main bundle targets an empty container entry node (like `<div id='root'>`) inside `index.html` and uses `createRoot` to render the root React tree into it dynamically."],
["What is the difference between React and Angular?", "React is an unopinionated client-side library focusing strictly on the view layer, utilizing a Virtual DOM. Angular is a full-featured, opinionated framework featuring built-in modules for routing and forms using a real DOM architecture."],
["What are other 5 JS frameworks other than React?", "Vue.js, Angular, Svelte, SolidJS, and Ember.js."],
["Whether React is a Framework or a Library? What is the difference?", "React is a Library. A library acts as a single tool you import to handle specialized tasks (you control the architecture flow). A framework dictates the global operational guidelines and handles the system flow, calling your code into its system configuration."],
["How React provides Reusability and Composition?", "Reusability is delivered by packaging standalone markup and logic into isolated component definitions. Composition allows components to accept other dynamic components as properties or layout data through the nested `children` prop."],
["What are State, Stateless, Stateful and State Management terms?", "State is localized mutable reactive component storage. Stateless refers to pure presentational components relying strictly on props. Stateful components track internal interaction metrics. State Management spans global solutions sharing structured data objects application-wide."],
["What are Props n JSX?", "Props are read-only properties passed down from a parent component to a child component, resembling standard element attributes inside custom JSX markup tags."],
]],
[3, "React Project - Main Files & Folders", [
["What is NPM? What is the role of node_modules folder?", "NPM (Node Package Manager) acts as the dependency registry repository. The `node_modules` subfolder physically houses the downscaled build resources and code file repositories fetched for external configuration tools and dependencies."],
["What is the role of public folder in React?", "The public folder serves static assets (like icons, robots.txt, or graphics) that are served directly unchanged without going through the compiler build engine pipelining."],
["What is the role of src folder in React?", "The src (source) folder acts as the workspace root directory containing all your working components, style rulesets, logic modules, and application structural configurations targeted by compilation assets."],
["What is the role of index.html page in React?", "It serves as the singular entry boilerplate page framework that provides the root container container anchor node used to bind the entire running script runtime architecture tree."],
["What is the role of index.js file and ReactDOM in React?", "It serves as the script runtime entry controller execution node. ReactDOM provides the operational mounting adapter used to inject compiled component trees into real browser DOM structures."],
["What is the role of App.js file in React?", "It functions as the core structural component root shell that coordinates top-level view routing components and shared system layouts."],
["What is the role of function and return inside App.js?", "The outer wrapper defines the structural presentation component engine logic block. The enclosed `return` statement hands down the operational layout layout instructions via target JSX templates."],
["Can we have a function without a return inside App.js?", "No. Valid components must deliver either a valid JSX element layout template structure, an array, a string representation data chunk, or explicit `null` flags to prevent layout render errors."],
["What is the role of export default inside App.js?", "It registers the root functional module definition as the primary public fall-through export accessible by layout imports elsewhere without strict destination object destructuring patterns."],
["Does the file name and the component name must be same in React?", "No, it is not strictly required by compiler systems, but it is deeply enforced as a clean architecture pattern to aid team troubleshooting and debugging navigation structures."],
]],
[4, "JSX - Questions", [
["What is the role of JSX in React? (3 points)", "1. Delivers intuitive declarative script layout formatting. 2. Automatically processes output content safely to guard against code scripts executing. 3. Enables JavaScript execution expressions cleanly using curly braces."],
["What are the 5 Advantages of JSX?", "1. Readable, HTML-like templates. 2. Enhanced compiler analysis catches syntax bugs early. 3. Highly optimized compiled assets. 4. Protects against security vulnerability vectors. 5. Keeps UI logic coupled with layout modules."],
["What is Babel?", "Babel is a modern JavaScript compilation compiler package system that translates developer files (like JSX blocks or modern ES6 code updates) into legacy ES5 execution scripts cross-compatible with older browser engines."],
["What is the role of Fragment in JSX?", "Fragments (<>...</>) allow you to group multiple child elements together under a parent branch wrapper layout without injecting unwanted DOM nodes like <div /> wrapper artifacts into your final page layout output."],
["What is Spread Operator in JSX?", "The spread operator ({...props}) spreads an object's properties directly as individual attribute assignments down to child destination template instances."],
["What are the types of Conditional Rendering in JSX?", "Standard conditional rendering patterns use JavaScript ternary blocks (condition ? TrueLayout : FalseLayout), short-circuit evaluations (condition && OutputLayout), or separate internal function block statements containing variable layouts."],
["How do you iterate over a list in JSX? What is map() method?", "Lists are rendered dynamically inside JSX using array iterations via the native .map() operator block, transforming single raw items inside an array input pool into target UI layout node segments assigned explicit unique key identifier properties."],
["Can a browser read a JSX File?", "No, browsers cannot interpret JSX directly. It must be processed and compiled into standard React.createElement nested JavaScript calls using packages like Babel before running on client systems."],
["What is Transpiler? What is the difference between Compiler & Transpiler?", "A transpiler transforms code between high-level formats with similar abstraction levels (e.g., JSX to JS). A compiler compiles higher-level human source script assets into lower-level instructions (e.g., Source scripts down to Machine assets)."],
["Is it possible to use JSX without React?", "Yes, modern configuration tools and bundlers allow you to point the JSX compilation output factory target toward other alternative libraries (like Preact or custom document rendering engines) using simple pragma adjustments."],
]],
[5, "Components - Functional/ Class", [
["What are React Components? What are the main elements of it?", "Reusable architectural chunks combining application state parameters and UI definitions. Their foundations rely upon explicit lifecycle patterns, prop mappings, internal memory hooks, and template definitions."],
["What are the Types of React components? What are Functional Components?", "The two main archetypes are Class Components and Functional Components. Functional Components are clean JavaScript functions that accept props as input parameters and return a layout segment, managing internals via Hooks."],
["How do you pass data between functional components in React?", "Data is passed downward from parents to children via unified layout props. Upward messaging relies upon parents passing down callback handlers that children execute to share data back up."],
["What is Prop Drilling in React?", "Prop drilling refers to threading state parameters through deeply nested layers of intermediate child components that do not actually need the data themselves, simply to deliver it to a deeply nested target node."],
["Why to Avoid Prop Drilling? In how many ways can avoid Prop Drilling?", "It reduces code readability and introduces brittle refactoring patterns. You can bypass it via standard Context API abstractions, component composition slots, or global application state storage engines like Redux."],
["What are Class Components In React?", "Class components are ES6 class structures extending React.Component, storing local state inside a this.state property instance object and using explicit lifecycle hook methods to manage side effects."],
["How to pass data between class components in React?", "Similar to functional variants, data maps downward through explicit target configuration props, accessed within child class definitions via the unified reference parameter token this.props."],
["What is the role of this keyword in class components?", "The this keyword points to the specific instantiated component instance context wrapper, granting access to properties like state tracker caches (this.state), incoming props configuration elements (this.props), and internal class methods."],
["What are the 5 differences btw Functional components and Class components?", "1. Functional variants rely on standard function statements; Class variants require full ES6 declarations. 2. Hooks track variables in functional components; a centralized this.state store acts as state container inside classes. 3. Functional components do not have lifecycles, relying on useEffect; Class variants provide dedicated methods like componentDidMount. 4. Functional blocks bypass instantiation allocations for faster runtimes. 5. Clean, functional code helps cut out complex runtime keyword setups (this bindings)."],
]],
[6, "Routing", [
["What is Routing and Router in React?", "Routing tracks navigational states in single-page apps. The Router acts as the top-level orchestration container that listens to changes in browser address configurations and coordinates layout rendering."],
["How to Implement Routing in React?", "You pull in the react-router-dom library, wrap your core setup under an access container (like BrowserRouter), and structure route options via component mapping lines (<Route path='/target' element={<Target />} />)."],
["What are the roles of <Route> & <Link> component in React Routing?", "The <Route> block maps a target URL to a specific element layout. The <Link> component replaces native anchor tags (<a>), preventing manual page-reload loops during view changes."],
["What are Route Parameters in React Routing?", "Dynamic URL parameter fields (/users/:id) used to pass variable arguments inside matching application route configurations, accessed inside active views via the custom hook parameter extractor useParams()."],
["What is the role of Switch Component in React Routing?", "In legacy React Router (v5), the <Switch> element iterates down through its child <Route> routes to render strictly the first direct match path it encounters. In modern versions (v6+), it is replaced by <Routes>."],
["What is the role of exact prop in React Routing?", "The exact prop guarantees that a route path will execute if, and only if, its configuration matching criteria matches the active URL query string precisely, preventing partial fall-through match errors on short path variables."],
]],
[7, "Hooks -useState/ useEffect", [
["What are React Hooks? What are the Top React Hooks?", "Hooks are specialized runtime functions that enable functional components to tap into core state and lifecycle features. Top hooks include useState, useEffect, useContext, useReducer, useMemo, useCallback, and useRef."],
["What are State, Stateless, Stateful and State Management terms?", "State is localized dynamic data. Stateless refers to UI components driven entirely by parameters. Stateful indicates components managing interactive data caches. State Management refers to application-wide state orchestration."],
["What is the role of useState() hook and how it works?", "It registers a reactive state variable within functional scopes. It returns an array containing two elements: the current state value cache and an update dispatcher function used to update that state and trigger a component re-render."],
["What is the role of useEffect(). How it works and what is its use?", "It serves as the side effect manager inside functional components, handling operations like API connections, subscription observers, or manual DOM adjustments after rendering completes."],
["What is Dependency Array in useEffect() hook?", "An array of variables passed as the second argument to useEffect. The hook monitors these values and re-runs its internal callback logic only when one of these variables changes between renders."],
["What is the meaning of the empty array [] in the useEffect()?", "An empty dependency array indicates that the side effect has no tracking references. This forces the block to run exactly once when the component is initially mounted, mimicking componentDidMount."],
]],
[8, "Hooks - useContext/ useReducer", [
["What is the role of useContext() hook?", "It allows a component to consume values from a React Context object directly, bypassing prop-drilling entirely."],
["What is createContext() method? What are Provider & Consumer properties?", "createContext() initializes a Context asset structure. The Provider is a component wrapper that delivers the shared value payload downstream. The Consumer is a legacy wrapper used to access those values via render props."],
["When to use useContext() hook instead of props in real applications?", "Use it when tracking application-wide parameters that must be accessed by many distant, nested components, such as visual theme states, current user details, or localized language rules."],
["What are the similarities between useState() and useReducer() hook?", "Both track local state adjustments, trigger component re-renders when data updates, and follow predictable, deterministic state updates."],
["What is useReducer() hook? When to use useState() and when useReducer()?", "useReducer manages state via a state reducer pattern. Use useState for simple standalone primitives; use useReducer when handling complex state objects with deeply nested updates or interdependent action conditions."],
["What are the differences between useState() and useReducer() Hook?", "useState updates values by directly passing a new state. useReducer handles updates indirectly by dispatching action objects ({ type: 'ACTION' }) to a central reducer function that computes the next state state layer."],
["What are dispatch & reducer function in useReducer Hook?", "The dispatch function triggers an action event payload. The reducer is a pure function that takes the current state and the incoming action object as inputs, evaluates them, and returns a new state object."],
["What is the purpose of passing initial state as an object in UseReducer?", "It groups multiple related slice tracks together under a structured state container, allowing the reducer to handle complex, interdependent field changes cleanly."],
]],
[9, "Hooks - useCallback/ useMemo/ useRef/ useLayoutEffect", [
["What is the role of useCallback() hook in React?", "It caches a function definition across component re-renders. This optimization prevents unnecessary rendering cycles in child components that rely on shallow reference checks for their props."],
["What parameters does the useCallback hook accept & what does it returns?", "It accepts an inline function block as its first argument and a dependency tracking array as its second. It returns a memoized version of the function reference."],
["What is the role of useMemo() Hook?", "It caches the computed output value of an expensive calculation, recalculating it only when variables listed in its dependency array change."],
["What is the role of useRef() Hook?", "It creates a mutable container object whose .current property persists across renders without triggering a component re-render when its value changes. It is commonly used to access DOM elements directly."],
["What is useLayoutEffect() Hook? Compare it with useEffect() hook.", "useLayoutEffect runs synchronously after all DOM modifications are made but before the browser paints the screen. useEffect runs asynchronously after the visual paint cycle completes."],
["When to use useLayoutEffect() Hook?", "Use it when measuring DOM element sizes, layout positions, or applying direct DOM changes that must happen seamlessly before the screen updates to prevent layout flickering."],
]],
[10, "Hooks - Best Practices/ Short Answer", [
["What are the Rules or Best Practices for hooks implementation?", "1. Call Hooks only at the top level of your components (never inside loops, conditions, or nested functions). 2. Call Hooks only from React function components or custom hooks."],
["What are Custom Hooks?", "Custom hooks are reusable JavaScript functions prefixed with use. They let you extract component logic into standalone, testable functions that can share stateful logic across multiple components."],
["What useState() hook return in React?", "It returns a stateful pair array containing the current state value at index 0 and a state update dispatcher function at index 1."],
["Tell 3 scenarios in which you can use useEffect() hook?", "1. Fetching data from an external backend API on mount. 2. Setting up and tearing down browser event listeners. 3. Synchronizing local state modifications with browser storage sync."],
["How can you conditionally run effects with useEffect?", "You cannot wrap the hook itself in a conditional statement. Instead, place your conditional logic inside the useEffect callback function block."],
["What problem do React Hooks solve?", "They solve the issue of reuse patterns and nested wrapper hell seen in class components, allowing you to share stateful logic across components without changing your component hierarchy."],
["What is the advantage of React Hooks over Lifecycle methods?", "They let you split up unrelated logic into small, isolated functions based on what pieces are doing (like setting up a subscription), rather than forcing unrelated code to crowd into lifecycle methods like componentDidMount."],
["What is Context API?", "A built-in state transmission architecture that provides a way to pass data down the component tree without having to drill props manually through every level."],
["What are the uses of all the Hooks in React?", "They provide a unified, composable way to manage state, handle side effects, consume context values, optimize performance, and interact directly with DOM nodes within functional components."],
]],
[11, "Components LifeCycle Methods - I", [
["What are Component life cycle phases?", "The three primary phases are: 1. Mounting (inserting into the DOM), 2. Updating (handling prop or state changes), and 3. Unmounting (removing from the DOM)."],
["What are Component life cycle methods?", "Specialized methods built into class components that execute automatically at specific milestones during a component's lifecycle (e.g., componentDidMount, componentDidUpdate)."],
["What are Constructors in class components? When to use them?", "The constructor is a special function called before a component mounts. It is used to initialize local state and bind event handlers to the class instance."],
["What is the role of super keyword in constructor?", "It calls the constructor of the parent class (React.Component). This initialization step must run before you can safely use the this keyword inside your own class constructor block."],
["What is the role of render() method in component life cycle?", "It is a mandatory pure function that examines this.props and this.state to return the JSX layout defining what should be rendered on the screen."],
["How the State can be maintained in a class component?", "State is initialized as an object inside the class constructor (this.state = {}) and updated using the specialized method this.setState(), which triggers a re-render."],
["What is the role of componentDidMount() method in component life cycle?", "It executes immediately after a component is inserted into the DOM tree. This is the ideal place to run setup tasks like fetching data from an API or setting up subscriptions."],
]],
[12, "Components LifeCycle Methods - II", [
["What is the role of componentDidUpdate() method in component life cycle?", "It runs immediately after a component updates due to state or prop changes. It is commonly used to perform DOM operations or make network requests based on a comparison of old and new data values."],
["What is the role of componentWillUnmount() method in component life cycle?", "It runs immediately before a component is destroyed and unmounted from the DOM. Use it to perform necessary cleanup, such as clearing timers or canceling network requests."],
["How do you initialize state in a class component?", "You can initialize state inside the class constructor by assigning an object to this.state, or use modern class fields syntax to declare state = {} directly inside the class body."],
["In which lifecycle phase component will be re-rendered?", "A component re-renders during the Updating phase, which is triggered when new props are received, setState() is called, or forceUpdate() is invoked."],
["What will happen if you don't define a constructor in your React component?", "React automatically injects a default base constructor behind the scenes. If you don't need to initialize local state or bind methods, you can omit the constructor entirely."],
["Why we need class components when we already have functional components?", "While modern functional components cover almost all use cases, class components are still needed for edge cases like using Error Boundaries (which require componentDidCatch). They are also common when maintaining older codebases."],
["What are the 5 main methods of component lifecycle?", "1. constructor(), 2. render(), 3. componentDidMount(), 4. componentDidUpdate(), and 5. componentWillUnmount()."],
]],
[13, "Controlled & Uncontrolled Components", [
["What are Controlled Components in React?", "Controlled components are input forms where the form data is handled directly by the React component's local state, making React the single source of truth for the input values."],
["What are the Differences btw Controlled & Uncontrolled Components?", "Controlled components store their values in React state and update via change handlers. Uncontrolled components let the traditional DOM maintain the form values, which you access using React Refs when needed."],
["What are characteristics of controlled components?", "They offer predictable data flows, allow for instant inline input validation, enable you to easily enforce custom input formatting, and support dynamic conditional form rendering."],
["What are the advantages of using controlled components in React forms?", "They make it easy to inspect and validate user input on every keystroke, allow you to control values programmatically, and simplify operations like disabling submit buttons until form fields are valid."],
["How to handle forms in React?", "Bind each input's value attribute to a state variable, update that state using an onChange event listener, and handle form submission using an onSubmit handler that calls event.preventDefault()."],
["How can you handle multiple input fields in a controlled form?", "Use a single shared event handler function that uses the input element's name attribute to update your state dynamically: this.setState({ [event.target.name]: event.target.value })."],
["How do you handle form validation in a controlled component?", "Evaluate input values directly within your onChange change handlers or during submission, saving any validation error messages into your state to display them conditionally in your UI."],
["In what scenarios might using uncontrolled components be advantageous?", "They are useful when integrating with non-React third-party DOM libraries, building simple forms with minimal validation requirements, or optimizing performance for large, complex forms where re-rendering on every keystroke becomes a bottleneck."],
]],
[14, "Code Splitting", [
["What is Code Splitting in React?", "A performance optimization technique that breaks a large compiled JavaScript bundle into smaller bundles. These smaller pieces can then be loaded dynamically on demand when a user visits specific routes or views."],
["How to Implement Code Splitting in React?", "You can implement code splitting using dynamic imports via the lazy() function and wrapping those lazy components inside a <Suspense> wrapper component."],
["What is the role of Lazy and Suspense methods in React?", "React.lazy() lets you render a dynamic import as a regular component. <Suspense> lets you specify a loading indicator to show while the browser fetches the lazy component's code bundle in the background."],
["What are the Pros and Cons of Code Splitting?", "Pros include faster initial page load times and reduced bandwidth usage. Cons include added architectural complexity and brief loading transitions as users navigate to new, un-downloaded parts of the app."],
["What is the role of the import() function in code splitting?", "It triggers a built-in promise-based feature that tells the bundler to split that specific file into a separate chunk, which the browser downloads dynamically at runtime."],
["What is the purpose of the fallback prop in Suspense?", "It accepts any valid React element (like a spinner or skeleton loader layout) to show on the screen while the app finishes downloading the dynamic background bundle files."],
["Can you dynamically load CSS files using code splitting in React?", "Yes. Modern bundlers automatically detect dynamic imports inside component files, splitting off the associated CSS modules into separate files that load alongside the component."],
["How do you inspect and analyze the generated chunks in a React application?", "You can use visual bundle analysis tools like source-map-explorer or rollup-plugin-visualizer to see a breakdown of your final production build files."],
]],
[15, "React - Others", [
["What is a Higher-Order Component in React?", "A advanced design pattern where a function takes a component as an argument and returns an enhanced component with injected props or extra functionality."],
["What are the 5 ways to Style React components? Explain Inline Styles?", "1. Inline Styles, 2. Global CSS stylesheets, 3. CSS Modules, 4. CSS-in-JS libraries (Styled Components), and 5. Utility frameworks (Tailwind). Inline styles use JavaScript objects passed directly to elements via the style={{}} attribute using camelCase keys."],
["What are the differences between React & React Native?", "React targets web development and renders components using standard virtual browser DOM nodes. React Native targets mobile development and compiles components down to native iOS and Android UI elements."],
["What is GraphQL?", "A query language for APIs that lets clients request exactly the specific data fields they need, reducing over-fetching and combining data requests into a single network call."],
["What are the Top 3 ways to achieve state management? When to use what in React?", "1. Local State (useState) for simple, component-specific data. 2. Context API for medium-scale apps with infrequent data updates. 3. Redux Toolkit for complex, large-scale apps with frequent, high-performance data operations."],
["How can you Implement Authentication in a React application?", "Configure an AuthContext to manage the user's status, use secure HttpOnly cookies for token storage, protect specific application paths using customized route wrappers, and clear all client states on logout."],
["What is the use of React Profiler?", "A built-in diagnostic panel tool that measures how often a component renders and highlights performance bottlenecks to pinpoint unnecessary rendering lag."],
["What is the difference between fetch & axios for api calls in React?", "fetch is a basic, built-in browser API that requires manual step conversions for JSON data. axios is an external library that automatically parses data, simplifies configuration defaults, and supports request interceptors out of the box."],
["What are the popular Testing Libraries for React?", "React Testing Library (RTL) for rendering and validating user interactions, alongside modern testing frameworks like Jest or Vitest."],
["How can you Optimize Performance in a React application?", "Implement code splitting with React.lazy, memoize component structures using React.memo, avoid inline object declarations inside loops, and trim down large external production dependencies."],
["Explain Reactive Programming with example?", "A programming paradigm focused on data streams and change propagation. For example, spreadsheet equations =A1+B1 are reactive: changing cell A1 automatically recalculates the final cell total instantly."],
["In how many ways can we implement Reactive Programming in React?", "You can manage reactive flows using specialized external event streams like RxJS, or rely on React's built-in hooks architecture (useState/useEffect) to propagate state changes automatically."],
["How to pass data from child component to parent Component in React?", "The parent component passes a handler function down to the child as a prop. The child component then executes this function, passing any relevant data back up as arguments."],
]],
[16, "Redux - Component/ Action/ Store/ Reducer", [
["What is the role of Redux in React?", "It serves as a predictable state container for the entire application, centralizing state management to make data sharing across far-apart components clean and easy to maintain."],
["When to use Hooks and when to use Redux in React applications?", "Use built-in hooks for local UI states (like toggling a modal open or closed). Use Redux for global data shared across distinct page routes, such as user shopping carts or cached profile authentication flags."],
["What is the Flow of data in React while using Redux?", "Redux follows a strict unidirectional data flow: A UI component dispatches an action object -> Reducers catch the action and update the central store -> The store updates the UI components with the new state."],
["How to install Redux for React application?", "Install the modern recommended packages by running npm install @reduxjs/toolkit react-redux in your project terminal."],
["What are Action Creators in React Redux?", "Action creators are helper functions that accept parameters and return a properly formatted action object containing a descriptive type string and an optional data payload."],
["Difference between Action Creators, Action Object & Action Type?", "The Action Type is a unique string identifying the operation. The Action Object is the resulting configuration bundle ({type, payload}). The Action Creator is the function that builds and returns that object."],
["Explain React Component Structure while using Redux?", "Components read slice data from the store using the useSelector hook and trigger state modifications by dispatching action objects using the useDispatch hook."],
["What is the role of Store in React Redux?", "The store acts as the single central database repository for your entire application's global state tree, providing methods to read data and dispatch actions."],
["What is the role of Reducer in Redux?", "A pure reducer function inspects the current state state tree and the incoming action, calculates the necessary updates safely, and returns a new state object without mutating the original state."],
]],
[17, "Redux - Core Principles/ Pros-Cons/ Local & Redux State", [
["Explain the Core Principles of Redux?", "1. Single Source of Truth (one global store). 2. State is Read-Only (can only be modified by dispatching actions). 3. Changes are made with Pure Functions (reducers)."],
["List 5 benefits of using Redux in React?", "1. Centralized global state management. 2. Predictable, deterministic state changes. 3. Powerful debugging with time-travel dev tools. 4. Code structure patterns that are easy to scale. 5. Simplified data sharing across distant components."],
["What are the differences between local component state & Redux state?", "Local state is self-contained and isolated within a single component file. Redux state is stored globally, completely detached from the UI tree, making it accessible from any component file across your app."],
["What are the challenges or disadvantages while using Redux?", "It can introduce a lot of initial boilerplates setup overhead, requires a shift in how you think about data flows, and can add unnecessary complexity to smaller applications that don't need a heavy state engine."],
]],
[18, "Redux - Short Answer", [
["What is Provider Component? How components getting the state from Redux store?", "The <Provider> component wraps your main App tree and injects the global Redux store, making it available to any nested components via custom hooks like useSelector."],
["What is the role of Connect function in React-Redux?", "A legacy higher-order function used in class components to map Redux state and action dispatches directly onto a component's props. Modern functional components use hooks instead."],
["What are the 4 Important Files in React-Redux project?", "1. store.js (global configuration), 2. slice.js (reducers and actions), 3. index.js (injecting the Provider), and 4. UI Component files."],
["How to structure the project and maintain state in multiple components?", "Organize your project by feature folders using the Redux Toolkit Slice model, combining action creators and reducers into independent, domain-specific state chunks."],
["Explain the concept of immutability in the context of Redux?", "Immutability means you never modify the original state object directly. Instead, you create and return an entirely new copy of the state object with the updated values, which helps prevent bugs and ensures predictable data tracking."],
["Which are the typical properties of an Action object in React-Redux project?", "A mandatory type property string describing the action, and an optional payload property containing any new data values needed for the update."],
["Difference btw mapDispatchToProps & mapStateToProps in the connect?", "mapStateToProps maps specific global store state values onto a component's props. mapDispatchToProps passes action dispatch helper functions onto props so the component can trigger updates."],
["What is the meaning of Unidirectional Data Flow in Redux?", "It means data moves strictly in one direction through a closed loop: UI actions trigger dispatcher calls -> Reducers calculate updates -> The Store alerts the UI, preventing messy, unpredictable state synchronization loops."],
["How does Redux handle communication between components?", "Components do not communicate with each other directly. Instead, they share data by dispatching updates to the central global store, which automatically pushes those changes out to any other components listening for them."],
["What is Payload property in Redux?", "The payload is the optional data package attached to an action object, providing the raw information the reducer needs to compute the next state updates."],
]],
[19, "Redux - Thunk/ Middleware/ Error Handling/ Flux", [
["What is the difference between Regular Action creator & Thunk action creator?", "Regular action creators return a simple JavaScript object instantly. Thunk action creators return an asynchronous function closure that has access to dispatch and getState, allowing you to make API calls before dispatching an update."],
["Explain the concept of Middleware in React-Redux?", "Middleware acts as a customizable pipeline extension that intercepts actions after they are dispatched but before they reach the reducers. This makes it the ideal place to handle logging, crash reporting, or async operations."],
["How can you handle Asynchronous Operations & side-effect in React-Redux?", "You handle asynchronous side effects using middleware tools like Redux Thunk or Redux Saga, which let you manage network requests cleanly outside of your pure reducer functions."],
["How does Error Handling work in Redux?", "Wrap your async operations inside try/catch blocks inside your thunks, and dispatch specific failure action objects ({type: 'FETCH_ERROR', payload: error}) to save error messages into your state and show them in the UI."],
["What is the difference between Flux & Redux?", "Flux is an architectural pattern that features multiple independent data stores and a single global dispatcher router. Redux streamlines this pattern into a single global store container that routes data through pure reducer functions without a standalone dispatcher."],
]],
[20, "JavaScript Essentials for React", [
["What are variables? What is the difference between var, let, and const?", "Variables are named data storage containers. var is function-scoped and hoisted, which can lead to unpredictable bugs. let and const are block-scoped; let allows you to reassign values later, while const creates a read-only variable assignment."],
["What are the types of conditions statements in JS?", "Standard conditional options include if/else statements, switch case routing blocks, and inline evaluation tools like ternary operators (condition ? true : false)."],
["What is Error Handling in JS?", "The mechanism used to catch and handle runtime exceptions using try, catch, finally, and throw blocks, preventing the application script from crashing when errors occur."],
["What is the difference between Spread and Rest operator in JS?", "Both use the same ... syntax. The spread operator spreads array or object elements out into individual values. The rest operator collects multiple separate arguments together into a single unified array structure."],
["What are Arrays in JS? How to get, add & remove elements from arrays?", "Arrays are ordered lists of data values. You access elements using numerical index lookups (arr[0]). You can add or remove elements using built-in array methods like .push(), .pop(), .shift(), .unshift(), and .splice()."],
["What is Array Destructuring in JS?", "A clean syntax feature that lets you extract values from an array and assign them to individual variables in a single line of code: const [first, second] = array;."],
["What are Functions in JS? What are the types of function?", "Functions are reusable blocks of executable code. Main archetypes include standard function declarations, function expressions, anonymous callback functions, and arrow functions."],
["Difference between named and anonymous functions? When to use what?", "Named functions have an explicit name identifier and support hoisting. Anonymous functions lack a name and are typically used as inline event handlers or short callback arguments passed into other operations."],
["What is function expression in JS?", "A function expression is when you define a function and assign it directly to a variable name container: const myFunc = function() {};."],
["What are Arrow Functions in JS? What is it use?", "A modern, concise function syntax (() => {}) that automatically binds the surrounding contextual this reference, making it perfect for writing clean inline callbacks."],
["What are Callback Functions? What is it use?", "A callback function is a function passed into another function as an argument, which is then executed later inside that function to handle asynchronous operations or events."],
["What is Higher-order function in JS?", "A function that either takes one or more functions as arguments or returns a new function as its output result, such as .map(), .filter(), or .reduce()."],
["What are Pure and Impure functions in JS?", "Pure functions always return the exact same output when given the same inputs and cause zero side effects. Impure functions modify external variables or rely on outside data changes, making their outputs unpredictable."],
["What are template literals and string interpolation in strings?", "Template literals use backticks (`) to write multi-line strings easily. String interpolation uses the ${variable} syntax to insert JavaScript expressions and variables directly into the string."],
["What are Objects in JS?", "Objects are standalone structural collections that store data as unordered sets of key-value pairs, where keys are strings and values can be any valid data type."],
["What is the difference between an array and an object?", "Arrays are ordered collections indexed by sequential numbers, ideal for lists. Objects are unordered collections indexed by custom string keys, ideal for representing distinct items with properties."],
["How do you add or modify or delete properties of an object?", "You can add or modify properties using dot notation (obj.key = value) or bracket notation (obj['key'] = value). Properties are removed entirely using the delete keyword: delete obj.key;."],
["What is asynchronous programming in JS? What is its use?", "A non-blocking execution model that lets your app kick off long-running operations (like network requests) and keep running other code in the meantime, handling the results whenever the task completes."],
["What is the difference between synchronous and asynchronous programming?", "Synchronous operations run strictly step-by-step, blocking any further code execution until the current line finishes. Asynchronous operations run in the background, letting the main code pipeline continue running without interruption."],
["What are Promises in JavaScript?", "A Promise is an object representing the eventual completion (or failure) of an asynchronous operation, existing in one of three states: Pending, Fulfilled, or Rejected."],
["How to implement Promises in JavaScript?", "Create a new instance using the Promise constructor: new Promise((resolve, reject) => {}), and handle the results downstream using .then() for success and .catch() for errors."],
["What is the purpose of async/ await? Compare it with Promises?", "A modern syntax wrapper built on top of Promises that lets you write asynchronous code that looks and reads like synchronous code, swapping out messy .then() chains for clean await expressions."],
["What are Classes in JS?", "Introduced in ES6, classes are syntactic sugar over JavaScript's existing prototype-based inheritance, providing a clean constructor layout to instantiate object models."],
["What is a constructor?", "A special initialization method inside a class definition block that executes automatically to set up state properties when creating a new class instance."],
["What is the use of this keyword?", "The this keyword points directly to the specific execution context object currently running the code block, dynamically changing its reference based on how and where the function was called."],
]],
[21, "Typescript", [
["What is Typescript? Or What is the difference between Typescript and Javascript?", "TypeScript is a strongly typed superset of JavaScript developed by Microsoft. It adds optional static typing to the language, catching type errors during development before the code is compiled down to standard JavaScript."],
["How to install Typescript and check version?", "Install it globally or locally via npm using npm install -g typescript, and verify your active compiler version by running tsc -v in your terminal."],
["What is the difference between let and var keyword?", "The same scoping rules apply as in JavaScript: var uses function-level scoping and supports variable hoisting, while let safely isolates variables within block scopes."],
["What is Type annotation?", "The explicit syntax pattern used to declare the expected data type of a variable, function parameter, or return value: let count: number = 10;."],
["What are Built in/ Primitive and User-Defined/ Non-primitive Types in Typescript?", "Primitive types are built-in basics like string, number, boolean, void, null, and undefined. Non-primitive types are user-defined structures like interfaces, classes, enums, and custom types."],
["What is 'any' type in Typescript?", "A fallback type flag that turns off static type checking for that variable, allowing it to hold any data type and essentially bypassing TypeScript's safety features for that value."],
["What is Enum type in Typescript?", "A feature that lets you define a clear set of named constants (like enum Directions { Up, Down, Left, Right }), making code more readable and self-documenting."],
["What is the difference between void and never types in Typescript?", "void means a function executes successfully but does not return a value. never means a function can never finish executing, either because it loops infinitely or throws a runtime error."],
["What is Type Assertion in Typescript?", "A way to tell the TypeScript compiler: I know what I'm doing, trust me on this data type, allowing you to cast a variable to a more specific type using the as keyword."],
["What are Arrow Functions in Typescript?", "Standard arrow functions with added type definitions for their parameters and explicit return types: const add = (x: number, y: number): number => x + y;."],
["What is Object Oriented Programming in Typescript?", "An architectural design pattern centered around modeling data as objects containing fields and methods, built using key features like classes, encapsulation, inheritance, and interfaces."],
["What are Classes and Objects in Typescript?", "A class acts as the blueprint framework specifying data fields and methods. An object is a live instance created from that class blueprint using the new keyword."],
["What is Constructor?", "A special function block inside a class that runs automatically when you instantiate a new object, used to assign initial values to class properties."],
["What are Access Modifiers in Typescript?", "Keywords that control the visibility of class properties and methods: public (accessible anywhere), private (accessible only inside that specific class), and protected (accessible within the class and its subclasses)."],
["What is Encapsulation in Typescript?", "The core OOP practice of bundling data fields and methods inside a single class, hiding internal implementation details using private access modifiers to guard data from unauthorized tampering."],
["What is Inheritance in Typescript?", "The mechanism where a child subclass inherits properties and methods from a parent superclass using the extends keyword, allowing you to reuse code efficiently."],
["What is Polymorphism in Typescript?", "The ability for different subclasses to implement the same inherited method in their own unique way, overriding the parent class's behavior to handle specialized tasks."],
["What is Interface in Typescript?", "A structural contract declaration that defines the exact shape an object must follow, listing mandatory property keys and types without writing the actual implementation code."],
["What's the difference between extends and implements in TypeScript?", "extends is used when a child class inherits from a parent class or when an interface inherits from another interface. implements is used when a class signs up to follow a structural contract defined by an interface."],
["Is Multiple Inheritance possible in Typescript?", "A class can only inherit from a single parent class using extends. However, a class can implement multiple structural interfaces at the same time using implements, letting you mimic multiple inheritance behavior safely."],
]],
];
